Keep one Vulkan descriptor pool and reset it, instead of one per dispatch

Every compute dispatch created a VkDescriptorPool sized for its kernel,
allocated one set, submitted, waited, and destroyed the pool: two driver
object round trips per dispatch, and a full-resolution render issues
hundreds of dispatches (each IIR blur alone is ceil(h/256) + ceil(w/256)
launches, patch 0001).

The backend now creates one pool on first use, sized for the widest kernel
(8 storage buffers, the most any entry in kKernels declares), and resets it
after every dispatch. That is safe for the same reason the old code could
destroy its pool there: dispatch holds dispatch_mutex_ for the whole call
and submit_sync waits for the queue to go idle, so the one set is never in
flight when the pool is reset. A kernel that ever declared more than 8
bindings is refused with a clear error rather than failing inside the
driver. The pool is destroyed with the device.

Output: unchanged by construction. Descriptor sets carry the same buffers
and ranges, recorded in the same command buffer; no shader, argument or
dispatch size changes.

